es":[{"parent":"Technology, Digital & Telecom","title":"Telecom & digital-infrastructure capex","tag":"Telecom-Infra","tier":1,"status":"Sharpest short-term","relevance":"","need":"5G monetization, FTTH and tower/fibre densification drive the connectivity cycle - and Q1FY27 repriced the optical leg outright. STLTECH booked order intake of INR 13,100cr in ONE quarter, 1.7x its entire FY26 order book, anchored by a multi-year $1.1bn hyperscaler deal running through FY29, taking the open order book to INR 18,618cr (up 2.4x QoQ) on revenue of INR 1,910cr (+87%) and EBITDA INR 397cr (+184%). It then raised guidance twice on the same call - EBITDA margin target from 20%-by-end-FY27 (already hit at 20.8% in Q1) to 23%, and Data-Center + Enterprise mix from 30% to 50% of FY27 revenue, after DC went from 1% of revenue in FY26 to 21%. The catch is supply, not demand: ex the anchor deal quarterly intake fell to ~INR 3,000cr from INR 7,000-8,000cr because 'we have to pick and choose the orders basis the capacity availability', with germanium and helium named as binding raw-material constraints. BHARTIARTL on ARPU + 5G; INDUSTOWER on tower rollout; SUYOG-tier on passive infra; GTPL/HFCL-tier on broadband + fibre; tariff hikes and rural FTTH are the near-term levers.","driver":"STLTECH's $1.1bn hyperscaler ramp against germanium/helium supply constraints and the raised 23% margin / 50% DC-mix guides; tariff-hike flow-through; 5G/FTTH capex; tower tenancy adds."}],"ipo":{"analysis":{},"summary":{}},"stageCoverage":null,"s":"ITI","meta":{"s":"ITI","co":"ITI LTD","isin":"INE248A01017","sector":"Telecommunication"},"identity":{"symbol":"ITI","name":"ITI LTD","sector":"Telecommunication","industry":"Telecom -  Equipment & Accessories","instrument_id":13007},"coverage":{"concall":false,"deepDive":false,"drhp":true,"projection":false,"stage":true},"pageModel":{"schemaVersion":"company-page/1.0","layout":"vertical","identity":{"symbol":"ITI","name":"ITI LTD","sector":"Telecommunication",